1. Open github.com and sign in.
2. Open a repository you can access (create one if needed).
3. Click Code → Codespaces → Create codespace on main (or choose the default branch).
4. Wait for the browser-based VS Code environment to load. This is VM1.
Step 2 — Create VM2
Return to the repository and create a second, independent Codespace using the same menu. Wait until it loads.
Keep both Codespaces available.
Step 3 — Open PowerShell and check GitHub CLI
On Windows, press Start, search for PowerShell, and open it. Run:
gh --version
Step 4 — Sign in to GitHub CLI
Check authentication first:
gh auth status
If you are not logged in, run the command below and follow the sign-in prompts:
gh auth login
Step 5 — Find your Codespace names
Run this in your computer's PowerShell (not inside a Codespace):
gh codespace list
Write down the actual names of VM1 and VM2. The names below are placeholders; do not type them literally.
Step 6 — Connect to VM1 and create the test file
Replace YOUR_VM1_NAME with the actual first Codespace name. Run:
gh codespace ssh -c YOUR_VM1_NAME
Inside VM1, run:
echo "This is a text file from Codespace1 to Codespace2" > ~/testfile.txt
Then return to your local PowerShell:
exit Experiment 6 • Page 2
Step 7 — Download the file from VM1 to your computer
Run this in local PowerShell. Replace YOUR_VM1_NAME with the actual name:
gh codespace cp -c YOUR_VM1_NAME 'remote:testfile.txt' ./testfile.txt
If successful, the command may finish without displaying a message.
Step 8 — Upload the file from your computer to VM2
Replace YOUR_VM2_NAME with the actual second Codespace name:
gh codespace cp -c YOUR_VM2_NAME ./testfile.txt 'remote:testfile.txt'
Step 9 — Verify the file inside VM2
Connect to VM2:
gh codespace ssh -c YOUR_VM2_NAME
Inside VM2, run these commands one at a time:
ls
cat ~/testfile.txt
Expected output: This is a text file from Codespace1 to Codespace2